    "validation_file": {
      "id": "2",
      "name": "test.py",
      "contents": "from unittest import TestCase\nfrom unittest_runner import ValidationProtocol\n\n\nclass TestPasswordChecker(TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        \"\"\"Run the student's code once and save the results\"\"\"\n        cls.stdout = ValidationProtocol().get_stdout_logs()\n        cls.accepted_lines = [line for line in cls.stdout if \"accepted\" in line.lower()]\n\n    def setUp(self):\n        if not self.accepted_lines:\n            self.skipTest(\"Nothing printed before your program reached input(). Make sure your required check at the top prints a result before you call input().\")\n\n    def test_prints_boolean_result(self):\n        \"\"\"Your required check prints True or False\"\"\"\n        self.assertTrue(\n            any(\"True\" in line or \"False\" in line for line in self.accepted_lines),\n            \"Make sure you're printing the Boolean variable itself (e.g. accepted), not just a sentence.\"\n        )\n\n    def test_rejects_matching_username(self):\n        \"\"\"Your required check correctly rejects a password that matches the username\"\"\"\n        self.assertIn(\n            \"False\", self.accepted_lines[0],\n            \"test_username and test_password are both \\\"trailblazer\\\" -- long enough, but identical to the username, so this should be False. If you got True, check whether you combined your rules with `and` -- using `or` here lets a matching password slip through.\"\n        )",
      "folderId": "0",
      "active": false,
      "type": "validation"
    },
    "long_instructions": "#Make: Build a Password Checker\r\n\r\n**Your Task:** Write a program that checks a username and password against a set of rules, then prints whether the password is accepted.\r\n\r\n**Your rules should:**\r\n\r\n- `len()` to check something about the password’s length (work with AI Tutor to learn about this)\r\n- At least one rule combining two conditions with `and` or `or`\r\n\r\n**Challenge:** Add a third rule grouped with parentheses, or rewrite one of your rules using `not`.\r\n\r\n**Important:** Never use a real password — make up fake credentials for testing.\r\n\r\n**Do This:**\r\n\r\n1. Fill out **MY PLAN** in `password_checker_plan.txt` before coding.\r\n- Write your rules with the `Required testing` section of `main.py` to test your logical operator use. \r\n- Write your rules again in the `Password Checker` section, using `username` and `password`.\r\n- Test your program with at least four username/password combinations, and record your predictions and results in **MY TESTING**.\r\n- Fill out **MY REFLECTION** in `password_checker_plan.txt`.",
    "teacher_markdown": "- `len()` is new — let the Documentation AI Tutor cover it on request. \r\n"
  },
